WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.359 --> 00:00:01.960
INTRO:
Remember Evergrande?

00:00:01.960 --> 00:00:07.300
In late September and early October of last
year, they were the star of financial news.

00:00:07.300 --> 00:00:11.599
Everyone was worried about what would happen
if this massive Chinese institution defaulted

00:00:11.599 --> 00:00:15.190
on their debt and the ripple effects that
it would have on the world.

00:00:15.190 --> 00:00:19.290
We saw headlines like “Fed warns China's
property problems could hurt global markets

00:00:19.290 --> 00:00:24.150
and the US economy” and “Wall Street marks
biggest drop since May as Evergrande crisis

00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:25.150
intensifies”.

00:00:25.150 --> 00:00:29.490
Yet, as the fed started raising interest rates
and the stock market started to sell off for

00:00:29.490 --> 00:00:34.390
other reasons, it seems that most people have
completely forgotten about this once monumental

00:00:34.390 --> 00:00:35.500
threat.

00:00:35.500 --> 00:00:39.450
This isn’t to say that the Evergrande situation
can be completely ignored though as the crisis

00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:41.440
has only gotten worse.

00:00:41.440 --> 00:00:46.700
In fact, at the end of last year, Evergrade
defaulted on $1.2 billion worth of foreign

00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:47.700
bonds.

00:00:47.700 --> 00:00:51.950
And as you would guess, their stock price
has only fallen further and further into abyss

00:00:51.950 --> 00:00:54.450
standing at just 17 cents today.

00:00:54.450 --> 00:00:59.559
So, whatever happened to the Evergrande crisis,
and does Evergrande even matter on a world

00:00:59.559 --> 00:01:00.610
scale?

00:01:00.610 --> 00:01:06.420
THE BIRTH OF EVERGRANDE:
Before we get into the potential consequences

00:01:06.420 --> 00:01:10.450
of the Evergrande crisis, let’s first take
a look at how this crisis even formed in the

00:01:10.450 --> 00:01:11.630
first place.

00:01:11.630 --> 00:01:15.200
This wasn’t something that just took place
over a matter of years.

00:01:15.200 --> 00:01:20.380
Rather, this issue has actually been brewing
for decades going all the way back to the

00:01:20.380 --> 00:01:21.380
1970s.

00:01:21.380 --> 00:01:25.560
You see, in 1978, the Chinese government adopted
a new economic policy that many scholars call

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:27.920
“China’s Open Door Policy”.

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:32.299
Before this ideology was adopted, China was
completely closed off to the world economy.

00:01:32.299 --> 00:01:37.210
They practiced an ideology called “zhong
xue wei ti, xi xue wei yong” which translates

00:01:37.210 --> 00:01:42.420
to "Chinese Learning as Substance, Western
Learning for Application" As the name suggests,

00:01:42.420 --> 00:01:45.909
China was intent on maintaining their traditional
culture as well as their communist political

00:01:45.909 --> 00:01:47.740
and economic system.

00:01:47.740 --> 00:01:52.220
However, they were willing to import technology
from the west and implement it into their

00:01:52.220 --> 00:01:53.220
society.

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:57.670
While this strategy worked decently for much
of the 1900s, it came with a slew of shortfalls.

00:01:57.670 --> 00:02:02.390
First of all, China was barely involved in
foreign trade, and secondly, no one wanted

00:02:02.390 --> 00:02:05.670
to invest in China which made it increasingly
difficult to keep up with the west.

00:02:05.670 --> 00:02:10.160
Not to mention, China’s economic and political
landscape had been devasted by the Cultural

00:02:10.160 --> 00:02:14.000
Revolution which took place between 1966 and
1976.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:18.989
So, in 1978, China finally gave in and adopted
the “Open Door Policy”.

00:02:18.989 --> 00:02:21.700
Things didn’t just change overnight though.

00:02:21.700 --> 00:02:25.160
China spent years deciding which sectors they
needed to control and which sectors they could

00:02:25.160 --> 00:02:26.450
loosen up.

00:02:26.450 --> 00:02:30.680
China was by no means willing to give up land
ownership, but they were willing to privatize

00:02:30.680 --> 00:02:34.819
how the land was used and this brings us to
the Amendment Act of 1988.

00:02:34.819 --> 00:02:38.760
This amendment made it possible for companies
and individuals to use land to their liking

00:02:38.760 --> 00:02:40.760
by obtaining land use rights.

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:44.500
This opened the door to a wide number of entrepreneurs
who were looking to build real estate and

00:02:44.500 --> 00:02:46.319
property development companies.

00:02:46.319 --> 00:02:50.129
As you would guess, Evergrande’s founder,
Xu Jiayin, fell under this category.

00:02:50.129 --> 00:02:55.220
Xu was actually a bit late to this trend as
he didn’t start Evergrande till 1996 which

00:02:55.220 --> 00:02:57.470
is 8 years after the amendment was adopted.

00:02:57.470 --> 00:03:01.660
Nonetheless, Evergrande would leverage this
new opportunity to the max and the company

00:03:01.660 --> 00:03:04.180
would grow exponentially over the next two
decades.

00:03:04.180 --> 00:03:08.410
Evergrande’s growth was only further accelerated
by new China joining the World Trade Organization

00:03:08.410 --> 00:03:09.879
in 2001.

00:03:09.879 --> 00:03:13.769
This made Chinese investments more appealing
than ever before which allowed Evergrande

00:03:13.769 --> 00:03:16.730
to grow an international base of investors
and stake holders.

00:03:16.730 --> 00:03:20.770
Ironically, Evergrande actually went public
during the height of the great financial recession

00:03:20.770 --> 00:03:22.959
in October of 2009.

00:03:22.959 --> 00:03:27.690
But despite this, they were still able to
raise $722 million which just goes to show

00:03:27.690 --> 00:03:31.090
how much interest their was in Chinese real
estate amongst international investors.

00:03:31.090 --> 00:03:34.930
All of these factors would allow Evergrade
to become one of the largest property developers

00:03:34.930 --> 00:03:37.860
in China and one of the biggest companies
in world.

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:44.430
In fact, the company was and still is part
of the Global 500 coming in at rank 122.

00:03:44.430 --> 00:03:46.830
This monumental growth didn’t come for free
though.

00:03:46.830 --> 00:03:51.099
Evergrande was constantly taking on more and
more risk which would eventually come to back

00:03:51.099 --> 00:03:53.110
haunt them.

00:03:53.110 --> 00:03:57.840
GHOST CITIES:
One of the biggest risks of the Chinese real

00:03:57.840 --> 00:04:02.410
estate market over the past 2 decades has
been the ever growing real estate bubble.

00:04:02.410 --> 00:04:06.470
One of the most famous examples of this is
the dozens of ghost cities located across

00:04:06.470 --> 00:04:07.470
China.

00:04:07.470 --> 00:04:11.090
If you’re not familiar with China’s ghost
cities, these are essentially massive developments

00:04:11.090 --> 00:04:12.780
that never became inhabited.

00:04:12.780 --> 00:04:16.989
Oftentimes, you see dozens of skyscrapers
and buildings that are completely empty and

00:04:16.989 --> 00:04:18.869
sometimes not even finished.

00:04:18.869 --> 00:04:23.650
Now, you might be wondering, how in the world
do these ghost cities even form in the first

00:04:23.650 --> 00:04:24.650
place?

00:04:24.650 --> 00:04:28.279
Well, if we take a look at Chinese real estate
law, we’ll quickly begin to understand how

00:04:28.279 --> 00:04:29.860
exactly this happened.

00:04:29.860 --> 00:04:33.659
China separates real estate developments into
a few different categories starting with economically

00:04:33.659 --> 00:04:35.129
affordable housing.

00:04:35.129 --> 00:04:39.050
China requires that such projects are occupied
by the owner and these projects cannot be

00:04:39.050 --> 00:04:41.339
bought or sold as an investment.

00:04:41.339 --> 00:04:45.700
On top of this, real estate companies cannot
sell these properties for more than 5% over

00:04:45.700 --> 00:04:47.069
the construction cost.

00:04:47.069 --> 00:04:50.939
So, these projects aren’t particularly lucrative
or appealing.

00:04:50.939 --> 00:04:55.199
One category that is super appealing, however,
is commodity housing which can be bought and

00:04:55.199 --> 00:04:57.710
sold as an investment in most cases.

00:04:57.710 --> 00:05:01.470
And in China, there’s extraordinary demand
for real estate.

00:05:01.470 --> 00:05:06.150
Real estate is often thought of the best way
to grow generational wealth, so even many

00:05:06.150 --> 00:05:10.639
middle class families opt to buy 2nd and 3rd
properties as soon as they can afford it.

00:05:10.639 --> 00:05:15.569
Also, China has yet to experience a 2008 style
housing market collapse, so as far as the

00:05:15.569 --> 00:05:19.349
everyday Chinese person knows, real estate
prices only go up.

00:05:19.349 --> 00:05:22.880
And, honestly, this is not a bad bet.

00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:26.930
Even if there is a crash, it’s just a matter
of time until real estate recovers because

00:05:26.930 --> 00:05:29.009
people will always need land.

00:05:29.009 --> 00:05:32.589
But, here’s the problem, you can’t own
land in China.

00:05:32.589 --> 00:05:36.349
So, if you want a piece of real estate in
an area that you think will develop in 20

00:05:36.349 --> 00:05:40.099
to 30 years, you have to build something on
it right now.

00:05:40.099 --> 00:05:43.590
And when millions of Chinese people are thinking
the same thing, it doesn’t take long to

00:05:43.590 --> 00:05:46.240
garner enough demand to build an entire ghost
city.

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:51.629
So, while these cities are indeed mostly if
not completely empty, they’re also completely

00:05:51.629 --> 00:05:53.050
sold out.

00:05:53.050 --> 00:05:56.949
This has provided Chinese real estate developers
with unprecedented demand.

00:05:56.949 --> 00:06:01.150
But when all of your demand comes from investors
and not people who are looking for a primary

00:06:01.150 --> 00:06:05.310
residence, it just takes one market downturn
to obliterate your demand.

00:06:05.310 --> 00:06:11.530
TURNING A BLIND EYE:
But, wait a minute, there’s no way the Chinese

00:06:11.530 --> 00:06:13.509
government didn’t see what was happening
right?

00:06:13.509 --> 00:06:17.300
How did they not think to stop this unsustainable
practice?

00:06:17.300 --> 00:06:20.219
Well, the answer is two fold.

00:06:20.219 --> 00:06:23.380
First of all, local governments are super
incentivized to sell land rights to these

00:06:23.380 --> 00:06:24.680
development companies.

00:06:24.680 --> 00:06:29.029
You see, municipal governments only get keep
about 40% of the country’s tax revenue.

00:06:29.029 --> 00:06:31.659
The rest must be passed on to the CCP.

00:06:31.659 --> 00:06:35.469
However, local governments have to pay for
80% of their expenses.

00:06:35.469 --> 00:06:39.969
So, local governments have to do something
to overcome this funding disparity, and the

00:06:39.969 --> 00:06:43.479
easiest answer for them has been selling land
rights.

00:06:43.479 --> 00:06:48.580
In 2015, for instance, land transferring fees
accounted for 40-60% of local government revenue.

00:06:48.580 --> 00:06:53.060
This is not that surprising when you consider
that land sales brought in $438 billion for

00:06:53.060 --> 00:06:54.860
local governments in 2012.

00:06:54.860 --> 00:06:59.740
I don’t think I need to explain why local
governments were a fan of ghost cities, but

00:06:59.740 --> 00:07:00.949
what about the CCP.

00:07:00.949 --> 00:07:04.860
Surely, they could see how this was a massive
bubble waiting to blow up right?

00:07:04.860 --> 00:07:10.499
Well, they did, and they would eventually
take action, but now before letting the situation

00:07:10.499 --> 00:07:12.520
get too far out of hand.

00:07:12.520 --> 00:07:16.740
You see, the CCP was also massively incentivized
to turn a blind eye to these ghost cities.

00:07:16.740 --> 00:07:21.389
The CCP wasn’t directly pulling in cash
from land sales like local governments, but

00:07:21.389 --> 00:07:24.339
they were able to pump up the Chinese GDP.

00:07:24.339 --> 00:07:28.639
The Chinese real estate industry accounts
for 29% of the entire country’s GDP.

00:07:28.639 --> 00:07:32.319
So, letting these ghost cities pop up was
extremely beneficial in terms of catching

00:07:32.319 --> 00:07:35.149
up and beating the US in terms of GDP.

00:07:35.149 --> 00:07:39.119
At first, this wasn’t a big issue as real
estate developers were pretty responsible

00:07:39.119 --> 00:07:40.119
financially.

00:07:40.119 --> 00:07:43.979
But, it didn’t take long for greed to kick
for developers like Evergrande.

00:07:43.979 --> 00:07:47.419
They started taking out substantially more
debt, and their debt to equity ratio fell

00:07:47.419 --> 00:07:48.719
off a cliff.

00:07:48.719 --> 00:07:55.839
For example, in 2014, Evergrande had ¥112
billion in equity and ¥206 billion in assets.

00:07:55.839 --> 00:08:00.610
This gave them an extremely healthy debt to
equity ratio of 0.839 meaning that they had

00:08:00.610 --> 00:08:02.249
more equity than debt.

00:08:02.249 --> 00:08:07.949
By 2020, their equity had grown to ¥350 billion
which sounds good until you realize that their

00:08:07.949 --> 00:08:10.809
assets had grown to ¥2.3 trillion.

00:08:10.809 --> 00:08:14.909
This results in a super dangerous debt to
equity ratio of 5.57.

00:08:14.909 --> 00:08:18.729
If Evergrande was an average American, they
would’ve had to take out PMI or private

00:08:18.729 --> 00:08:22.599
mortgage insurance because they didn’t even
have 20% equity.

00:08:22.599 --> 00:08:25.639
This issue wasn’t just limited to Evergrande
either.

00:08:25.639 --> 00:08:28.879
Many Chinese real estate companies were in
similar positions which made it abundantly

00:08:28.879 --> 00:08:32.839
clear to the CCP that they had to take action.

00:08:32.839 --> 00:08:38.060
TOO LITTLE TOO LATE:
In August of 2020, Chinese regulators met

00:08:38.060 --> 00:08:42.229
with the country’s largest real estate companies
including Evergrande and they introduced three

00:08:42.229 --> 00:08:44.589
restrictions called the three red lines.

00:08:44.589 --> 00:08:48.470
These restrictions put caps on three debt
ratios which were debt to cash, debt to equity,

00:08:48.470 --> 00:08:50.100
and debt to assets.

00:08:50.100 --> 00:08:54.490
As you would guess, Evergrande didn’t exactly
fall within these restrictions, so they would

00:08:54.490 --> 00:08:56.960
start selling off properties and raising cash.

00:08:56.960 --> 00:09:01.509
For example, in September of 2020, they discounted
properties by 30% to increase sales.

00:09:01.509 --> 00:09:06.910
Similarly, in October, they raised $555 million
by completing a share offering.

00:09:06.910 --> 00:09:10.720
They also cancelled certain projects, but
none of this was enough to improve their financial

00:09:10.720 --> 00:09:12.279
situation substantially.

00:09:12.279 --> 00:09:17.029
Nonetheless, Evergrande was able to stay afloat
thanks to sufficient cashflow, but it didn’t

00:09:17.029 --> 00:09:19.720
take long for this to go downhill as well.

00:09:19.720 --> 00:09:24.480
In late 2020, Jack Ma and the CCP would get
into a bit of a conundrum which prompted the

00:09:24.480 --> 00:09:27.040
CCP to crackdown on Chinese tech companies.

00:09:27.040 --> 00:09:30.731
While most of the CCPs actions were directly
targetted towards tech companies, as tech

00:09:30.731 --> 00:09:33.540
stocks crumbled, so did investor confidence.

00:09:33.540 --> 00:09:37.139
And as investors started pulling back on real
estate investments, Evergrande’s net profit

00:09:37.139 --> 00:09:39.230
started to decline as well.

00:09:39.230 --> 00:09:44.250
In August of 2021, Evergrande reported a 29%
year over year decline in net profit.

00:09:44.250 --> 00:09:48.839
And given Evergrande’s already fragile position,
this was enough for default fears to go rampant.

00:09:48.839 --> 00:09:52.399
Evergrande continued to insist that while
they were facing issues, there was nothing

00:09:52.399 --> 00:09:53.399
to worry about.

00:09:53.399 --> 00:09:57.410
You could say that these were their famous
last words as Evergrande would miss their

00:09:57.410 --> 00:09:59.790
first set of coupon payments just a few weeks
later.

00:09:59.790 --> 00:10:04.290
Shortly after, Evergrande would begin missing
coupon payments on a regular basis until they

00:10:04.290 --> 00:10:08.970
eventually defaulted on $1.2 billion worth
of debt.

00:10:08.970 --> 00:10:14.260
CCP TO THE RESCUE:
The CCP has never publicly acknowledged that

00:10:14.260 --> 00:10:18.230
they’re bailing out Evergrande, but that’s
basically what they’ve been doing.

00:10:18.230 --> 00:10:22.160
They’re not exactly saving the company itself,
but they’re trying their best to ensure

00:10:22.160 --> 00:10:25.620
that the collapse of Evergrande does not lead
further consequences.

00:10:25.620 --> 00:10:29.600
And the main way their accomplishing this
is by nationalizing the company.

00:10:29.600 --> 00:10:33.540
For example, local governments have been seizing
rights granted to Evergrande.

00:10:33.540 --> 00:10:37.660
Similarly, China’s central bank freed up
$188 billion to help alleviate any liquidity

00:10:37.660 --> 00:10:40.180
problems caused by Evergrande’s default.

00:10:40.180 --> 00:10:43.519
Evergrande has also been selling parts of
their own business to raise cash and pay off

00:10:43.519 --> 00:10:44.519
debt.

00:10:44.519 --> 00:10:49.259
So, at this point, it does look like China
has Evergrande under control and that they’ll

00:10:49.259 --> 00:10:52.200
be able to contain the crisis, but only time
will tell.

00:10:52.200 --> 00:10:56.839
Do you think the Evergrande crisis is behind
us or is it just a preview as to what’s

00:10:56.839 --> 00:10:57.839
to come?

